         4. Frontend Architecture

The frontend is developed using React.

The frontend is responsible for:

Displaying the user interface.
Managing user interactions.
Collecting form input.
Performing client-side validation.
Sending requests to backend APIs.
Displaying API responses.
Managing authentication state.
Displaying loading, success, and error states.
Providing different interfaces for customers and administrators.
Main Frontend Pages
Login
Register
Dashboard
Products
Cart
Orders
Admin Panel
Frontend Components

Reusable components may include:

Navigation Bar
Product Card
Form Components
Buttons
Loading Indicators
Error Messages
Order Summary
Protected Route
5. Backend Architecture

The backend is developed using Node.js and Express.js.

The backend is responsible for:

Providing REST APIs.
Processing business logic.
Authenticating users.
Authorizing user roles.
Validating requests.
Communicating with PostgreSQL.
Managing products and inventory.
Processing carts and orders.
Generating invoice information.
Recording audit activities.
Handling errors.

The backend follows a modular structure so that individual responsibilities remain separated and maintainable.

6. Backend Components
6.1 Routes

Routes define the API endpoints available to the frontend.

Examples include:                
/api/auth
/api/users
/api/products
/api/cart
/api/orders
/api/admin
/api/invoices
/api/audit

6.2 Controllers

Controllers handle incoming requests and generate appropriate responses.

Examples:

Authentication Controller
Product Controller
Cart Controller
Order Controller
Admin Controller
Invoice Controller
Audit Controller
6.3 Middleware

Middleware performs common processing before a request reaches the main business logic.

Examples:

JWT Authentication Middleware
Role Authorization Middleware
Request Validation Middleware
Error Handling Middleware
6.4 Services

Service modules contain reusable business logic and help keep controllers organized.

Possible services include:

Authentication Service
Product Service
Inventory Service
Cart Service
Order Service
Invoice Service
Audit Service
7. Database Architecture

PostgreSQL will be used as the primary relational database.

The database will store structured information required for the operation of the platform.

Major entities include:

Users
Products
Inventory
Carts
Cart Items
Orders
Order Items
Invoices
Audit Activities

Relationships between these entities will be defined using primary keys and foreign keys.

8. Main Database Entities
8.1 Users

Stores customer and administrator account information.

Possible fields:

id
name
company_name
email
phone
password_hash
role
created_at
updated_at
8.2 Products

Stores information about products available on the platform.

Possible fields:

id
name
description
price
stock_quantity
status
created_at
updated_at
8.3 Carts

Stores customer cart information.

Possible fields:

id
user_id
created_at
updated_at
8.4 Cart Items

Stores products and quantities associated with a cart.

Possible fields:

id
cart_id
product_id
quantity
8.5 Orders

Stores customer order information.

Possible fields:

id
user_id
total_amount
status
created_at
updated_at
8.6 Order Items

Stores individual products included in an order.

Possible fields:

id
order_id
product_id
quantity
unit_price
subtotal
8.7 Invoices

Stores invoice-related information associated with an order.

Possible fields:

id
order_id
invoice_number
total_amount
generated_at
8.8 Audit Activities

Stores important system activities.

Possible fields:

id
user_id
action
entity_type
entity_id
description
created_at
9. Authentication Architecture

The platform will use JSON Web Token (JWT) based authentication.

The authentication flow is:

User Login
     ↓
Frontend sends credentials
     ↓
Backend validates credentials
     ↓
Password verification
     ↓
JWT token generated
     ↓
Token returned to frontend
     ↓
Frontend stores authentication state
     ↓
Token sent with protected API requests
     ↓
Backend validates token
     ↓
Request is authorized

Passwords must not be stored as plain text.

A secure password hashing mechanism will be used before storing passwords in the database.

10. Role-Based Access Control

The system will support role-based access control.

The primary roles are:

Customer
Administrator
Customer Access

Customers can:

View products
Manage their cart
Place orders
View their orders
Access their invoice information
Administrator Access

Administrators can:

Manage products
Manage inventory
Manage orders
Review audit activities
Access administrative functionality

Role verification will be performed on protected backend routes.

11. REST API Architecture

The frontend and backend will communicate using REST APIs.

Example API structure:

| Method | Endpoint                 | Purpose                 |
| ------ | ------------------------ | ----------------------- |
| POST   | `/api/auth/register`     | Register a user         |
| POST   | `/api/auth/login`        | Authenticate a user     |
| GET    | `/api/products`          | Get products            |
| POST   | `/api/products`          | Create a product        |
| PUT    | `/api/products/:id`      | Update a product        |
| DELETE | `/api/products/:id`      | Delete a product        |
| GET    | `/api/cart`              | Get current cart        |
| POST   | `/api/cart/items`        | Add item to cart        |
| PUT    | `/api/cart/items/:id`    | Update cart item        |
| DELETE | `/api/cart/items/:id`    | Remove cart item        |
| POST   | `/api/orders`            | Create an order         |
| GET    | `/api/orders`            | Get customer orders     |
| GET    | `/api/orders/:id`        | Get order details       |
| GET    | `/api/invoices/:orderId` | Get invoice information |

12. Request and Response Flow

A typical API request follows these steps:

User performs an action on the frontend.
React sends an HTTP request.
Express receives the request.
Authentication middleware verifies the user when required.
Authorization middleware verifies the user's role.
Validation middleware validates request data.
Controller processes the request.
Service layer performs business logic.
Database operations are performed.
Backend sends an HTTP response.
React processes the response.
The user interface is updated.
13. Validation Design

Validation will be implemented at multiple levels.

Frontend Validation

The frontend will validate:

Required fields
Email format
Password confirmation
Quantity values
Basic input constraints
Backend Validation

The backend will validate:

Request body data
User credentials
Product information
Stock quantities
Order information
Authorization requirements

Backend validation is required because client-side validation alone cannot provide sufficient security.

14. Error Handling Design

The system will provide consistent error handling.

Common error categories include:

Validation errors
Authentication errors
Authorization errors
Resource not found errors
Database errors
Server errors

The API should return appropriate HTTP status codes and clear error messages without exposing sensitive implementation details.

15. Inventory and Order Consistency

Inventory management is an important part of the order workflow.

Before creating an order:

The system checks product availability.
Requested quantity is compared with available stock.
The order is created only when sufficient stock is available.
Stock is updated after successful order creation.
The system should prevent invalid inventory values.

Database transactions may be used for operations where multiple related database changes must remain consistent.

16. Audit Activity Design

Important administrative actions should be recorded in the audit activity system.

Examples include:

Product creation
Product update
Product deletion
Inventory update
Order status update
Other important administrative operations

Audit records provide evidence of important system activities and support project review and accountability.

17. Security Design

The following security practices will be applied:

Password hashing
JWT-based authentication
Role-based authorization
Backend request validation
Protected API routes
Secure environment variables
No hard-coded database passwords
No hard-coded JWT secrets
Appropriate error responses
Protection of sensitive user information

Development credentials and secret configuration should not be committed to the public GitHub repository.


